# Compiler fixture input fidelity

Issue 305, Plane DECKO-757. Only the Bootstrap colorpicker input string is
corrected. Every captured compiler output and every other fixture field is
unchanged. The correction is an input-fidelity repair, not a new Ruby capture
or a change to expected behavior.

## Provenance and mechanism

Commit `77df2156d62dc9f96aa486cbdac5d2af5fc86fee` changed only
`compile_pins[1].source` among all compiler input/output fields. At byte 8804,
`rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2)` became `rhbq(0, 0, 0, 0.2)`; length stayed 13,640 bytes.
The compiled expectations did not change. The parent input SHA256 is
`27166b0e6b957e2f2295ffec37567f5464f781abc9b1dbd690ad2086122a6ace`,
identical to the existing approved asset and its independently retained
source profile. The changed input SHA256 was
`7ad885ae343fcea63f2282f481f0e2499faa44eebe9ae90f6b06daab72164f4f`.

The previous tests accept a compiler error for this source because its
standalone input lacks `$white`; both strings still throw. They therefore
cannot detect this input corruption. The new offline Node controls bind all
twelve stored compiler input strings to the admitted asset profile's exact
path, byte length and SHA256. Generic text editing can no longer change a
stored input unnoticed. This binds the declared historical snapshot, not
publisher authenticity or a complete source-supply audit.

The fixture was patched at that one verified token. A structural comparison
confirmed that restoring the old source field makes the complete before/after
JSON objects equal. No compiled expectation was regenerated or normalized.
Whole-fixture SHA256 changes from
`0eccb6284a28cb89b195401abc2230a9b105d13df723b803c777bec5ef590cbf` to
`eadd612caf450a605397815f567e29a754b988edff8d21b4abad228308859486`.

